# The law `rank/decomposition.sqlc` owes: cutting a graph by filing loses no edge.
#
# Without it the measurement cannot be read
#   That relation reports the unit graph's one loop across the whole corpus and none when the graph
#   is cut by filing, and concludes that the cut breaks a loop. It would report the same numbers if
#   the cut had simply dropped the edges, and the conclusion would then be about a fault rather than
#   about the graph. This law is what makes the difference structural: every edge appears once
#   across the corpus and once under its own filing, so whatever the cut loses is connections and
#   never edges.
#
# Why the union is `ALL`
#   The repeats are what matter. The corpus-wide branch and the per-filing branches count one edge
#   twice on purpose, so a `UNION` that removed repeats would compare a number with itself and show
#   nothing. `algebra/roster.sqlc` carries that distinction in its own `multiplicity` column, and
#   this row is `bag`.
#
# The number of graphs is counted
#   `graphs` is counted rather than written as a literal, because a literal would not move when a
#   third graph arrives. The claimant graph has no edges on the corpus, so a literal could be right
#   and still be unreadable: nothing would say whether it meant "this model composes two graphs"
#   or "two of them have edges".
-- rank/decomposition.sqlc's two scopes, counted: the whole against the sum of the parts.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'rank/decomposition' AS subject,
           x.whole = x.per_filing AS holds,
           format('%s edges across the corpus, %s added over %s filings, across %s graphs',
                  x.whole, x.per_filing, x.filings, x.graphs) AS detail
    FROM ( SELECT
             (SELECT count(*) FROM (
                 SELECT DISTINCT g.graph, g.from_node, g.to_node
                 FROM ( :compose(rank/graph_edges.sqlc) ) g) e)                      AS whole,
             (SELECT count(*) FROM (
                 SELECT DISTINCT g.graph, g.filing, g.from_node, g.to_node
                 FROM ( :compose(rank/graph_edges.sqlc) ) g) e)                      AS per_filing,
             (SELECT count(DISTINCT g.filing)
              FROM ( :compose(rank/graph_edges.sqlc) ) g)                            AS filings,
             (SELECT count(DISTINCT g.graph)
              FROM ( :compose(rank/graph_edges.sqlc) ) g)                            AS graphs
         ) x
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'decomposition'
